WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.662
This JSON record is self-describing because the keys tell us what each value represents.

00:00:05.662 --> 00:00:07.897
But it is not a flat table.

00:00:07.897 --> 00:00:14.127
Heart rate is nested inside metrics, and latitude and longitude are nested inside location.

00:00:14.127 --> 00:00:19.237
A second record could add oxygen saturation or omit sleep hours.

00:00:19.237 --> 00:00:24.052
Before analysis, we often flatten the JSON into columns.

00:00:24.052 --> 00:00:26.757
But flattening is only a structural step.

00:00:26.757 --> 00:00:33.482
We still need to understand units, missingness, and the analytical meaning of the extracted fields.

00:00:33.482 --> 00:00:38.577
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:38.577 --> 00:00:44.642
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:44.642 --> 00:00:47.267
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:47.267 --> 00:00:51.907
In a real setting such as an e-commerce team preparing customer, purchase, and web-event data,

00:00:51.907 --> 00:01:00.027
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:01:00.027 --> 00:01:02.267
before discussing syntax.

00:01:02.267 --> 00:01:08.640
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
